WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:14.009
On December 17 the real bank robber was arrested.

2
00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:21.405
On that day London opinion changed for the second time since the wager had begun—this time in Mr. Fogg's favor.

3
00:00:21.406 --> 00:00:26.735
The papers, which had presented him as a criminal, reported him an honorable gentleman again.

4
00:00:26.736 --> 00:00:32.260
They resumed their discussion about the wager, and once again, bets were placed.

5
00:00:32.261 --> 00:00:36.945
December 17 was the seventy-sixth day of the eighty-day race.

6
00:00:36.946 --> 00:00:41.919
Mr. Fogg's five friends at the Reform Club passed the remaining days in suspense.

7
00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:47.514
During the time when everyone thought he was a thief, Mr. Fogg's friends had not expected him to return.

8
00:00:47.515 --> 00:00:52.138
Now they realized the wager was still on. Where was Mr. Fogg?

9
00:00:52.139 --> 00:00:54.604
No news had been received from him.

10
00:00:54.605 --> 00:00:59.776
Was he dead? Had he given up? Was he continuing his journey?

11
00:00:59.777 --> 00:01:05.307
A huge crowd collected outside the Reform Club on the evening of Saturday, December 21.

12
00:01:05.308 --> 00:01:11.278
Traffic was interrupted, and arguments, discussions, and last-minute bets excited the crowd.

13
00:01:11.279 --> 00:01:16.956
As the hour approached when Phileas Fogg was due, the excitement rose to its highest pitch.

14
00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:20.255
The five friends waited in the great saloon of the club.

15
00:01:20.256 --> 00:01:27.544
When the clock indicated twenty minutes past eight, one member said, "What time did the last train arrive from Liverpool?"

16
00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:31.322
"At twenty-three minutes past seven," another answered.

17
00:01:31.323 --> 00:01:38.396
"Well, gentlemen, if Phileas Fogg had come on the 7:23 train, he would have gotten here by now.

18
00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:42.654
We can, therefore, regard the bet as won."

19
00:01:42.655 --> 00:01:48.480
"Wait. Let us not be too hasty. Mr. Fogg is an eccentric man.

20
00:01:48.481 --> 00:01:53.567
His punctuality is well known. He never arrives too soon or too late.

21
00:01:53.568 --> 00:01:57.216
I will not be surprised if he appears here at the last minute."

22
00:01:57.217 --> 00:02:01.397
Soon the hands on the clock pointed to twenty minutes to nine.

23
00:02:01.398 --> 00:02:04.457
"Five more minutes," someone noted.

24
00:02:04.458 --> 00:02:09.716
The five gentlemen looked at each other. Their anxiety was becoming intense.

25
00:02:09.717 --> 00:02:12.683
The clock advanced to eighteen minutes to nine.

26
00:02:12.684 --> 00:02:16.840
Then to seventeen minutes to nine. They all fell silent.

27
00:02:16.841 --> 00:02:19.870
Only the murmurs of the crowd outside could be heard.

28
00:02:19.871 --> 00:02:23.818
"Sixteen minutes to nine," one of the gentlemen noted.

29
00:02:23.819 --> 00:02:26.853
One more minute and the wager would be won.

30
00:02:26.854 --> 00:02:32.809
At the fifty-fifth second, a loud cry was heard from the street, followed by applause and cheers.

31
00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.425
The five men rose from their seats.

32
00:02:35.426 --> 00:02:39.443
At the fifty-seventh second, the door of the saloon opened.

33
00:02:39.444 --> 00:02:45.197
Phileas Fogg stepped in, followed by an excited crowd that forced its way through the club doors.

34
00:02:45.198 --> 00:02:50.596
In his usual calm voice, Mr. Fogg said, "Here I am, gentlemen."

35
00:02:50.597 --> 00:02:54.438
Yes, Phileas Fogg had won the bet. But how?

36
00:02:54.439 --> 00:02:59.539
Well, as instructed, Passepartout had hurried to Reverend Wilson's house.

37
00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:05.458
He was not at home, and Passepartout waited twenty minutes before the reverend strolled up to the door.

38
00:03:05.459 --> 00:03:12.202
They exchanged only a few words when Passepartout turned and ran as fast as he could!

39
00:03:12.203 --> 00:03:19.032
He leaped over vendors' carts, knocked over pedestrians, and in three minutes, was at Saville Row again.

40
00:03:19.033 --> 00:03:21.774
"What is the matter?" asked Mr. Fogg.

41
00:03:21.775 --> 00:03:27.666
"My master . . . marriage . . . impossible . . ." Passepartout gasped.

42
00:03:27.667 --> 00:03:30.416
"Impossible?" asked Fogg.

43
00:03:30.417 --> 00:03:32.751
"Impossible for tomorrow."

44
00:03:32.752 --> 00:03:33.938
"Why?"

45
00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:37.174
"Because tomorrow is not Monday!" cried Passepartout.

46
00:03:37.175 --> 00:03:42.339
"Tomorrow is Sunday, and today is Saturday! We have made a mistake.

47
00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:46.650
There are only ten minutes left! You must go!"

48
00:03:46.651 --> 00:03:51.364
Passepartout then grabbed his master's collar and dragged him out of the house.

49
00:03:51.365 --> 00:03:55.891
Mr. Fogg jumped into a cab and promised the cabman a hundred pounds.

50
00:03:55.892 --> 00:04:03.712
Ignoring the whistles of five traffic police and causing three accidents, the cabman delivered Fogg and his friends at the Reform Club.

51
00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:09.297
At a quarter before nine, Phileas Fogg had walked into the great saloon and won his wager!

52
00:04:09.298 --> 00:04:13.690
But where had he miscalculated? The cause of the error was simple.

53
00:04:13.691 --> 00:04:17.179
Phileas Fogg had been traveling constantly eastward.

54
00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:20.626
In journeying eastward he had gone toward the sun.

55
00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:26.634
Therefore, each day was shortened by four minutes for every degree of longitude he crossed.

56
00:04:26.635 --> 00:04:30.701
The circumference of the earth is measured in 360 degrees.

57
00:04:30.702 --> 00:04:35.820
Three hundred and sixty degrees multiplied by four is exactly 24 hours.

58
00:04:35.821 --> 00:04:39.264
Without realizing it Fogg had gained a day.

59
00:04:39.265 --> 00:04:45.528
That evening Mr. Fogg, as tranquil as ever, said to Aouda, "Is our marriage still agreeable to you?"

60
00:04:45.529 --> 00:04:49.270
"It is for me to ask that question," she said.

61
00:04:49.271 --> 00:04:52.667
"You were ruined, but now you are rich again."

62
00:04:52.668 --> 00:04:57.334
"Pardon me, madam," said Fogg. "But my fortune belongs to you.

63
00:04:57.335 --> 00:05:02.702
If you had not suggested our marriage, Passepartout would not have gone to the reverend's house—"

64
00:05:02.703 --> 00:05:05.474
"Dear Mr. Fogg!" she stopped him.

65
00:05:05.475 --> 00:05:08.662
"Dear Aouda!" he replied with his whole heart.

66
00:05:08.663 --> 00:05:12.260
Mr. Fogg and Aouda were married 48 hours later.

67
00:05:12.261 --> 00:05:18.146
Passepartout, glowing with pride, gave the bride away.

68
00:05:18.147 --> 00:05:21.753
Mr. Fogg had traveled around the world in eighty days.

69
00:05:21.754 --> 00:05:27.286
He had employed every means of vehicle, from trains and steamers to elephants and sledges.

70
00:05:27.287 --> 00:05:31.679
In the end he was no richer than when he set out. No richer?

71
00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.259
Perhaps, but he had gained a love that made him the happiest of men!

72
00:05:36.260 --> 00:05:42.281
Would you not travel around the world for a chance at the same?

73
00:05:42.282 --> 00:05:50.367
Hooray! Mr. Fogg won his wager! Thank goodness for those funny lines on maps—longitude and latitude.

74
00:05:50.368 --> 00:05:54.284
On a map, time zones are marked by lines of longitude.

75
00:05:54.285 --> 00:05:58.006
The lines divide the globe into sections, or time zones.

76
00:05:58.007 --> 00:06:04.267
Each section is one hour ahead of the section to its west and one hour behind the one to its east.

77
00:06:04.268 --> 00:06:10.220
Look closely at the map. Can you see the lines that run across the globe east to west?

78
00:06:10.221 --> 00:06:15.547
They are called lines of latitude. Do you know the most famous line of latitude?

79
00:06:15.548 --> 00:06:21.418
I'll give you a clue. It's very hot there. That's right–the equator!

80
00:06:21.419 --> 00:06:25.093
The equator runs right around the middle of the earth.

81
00:06:25.094 --> 00:06:28.169
Well, I must say good-bye to you now.

82
00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:39.956
I hope you've enjoyed traveling with Mr. Fogg and me around the world—it's been quite an adventure, hasn't it?
